878 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (To accurately monitor the effectiveness of water filters, the system employs a differential pressure sensor. This sensor features two pressure ports placed strategically before and after the filter. By measuring the inlet and outlet pressures, it can calculate the pressure difference. Based on this differential pressure and pre-determined laboratory values and assumptions, the system can determine whether the filter remains usable. This information is then conveyed to the user via an NFC tag. This choice of differential pressure to measure flow rate, rather than other parameters, is carefully considered: flow rate and the saturation of activated carbons are the only parameters that can reliably indicate filter effectiveness without requiring memory or a battery; activated carbons, which are widely used in water filters, absorb impurities through their micro-pores, once saturation is reached, they stop functioning effectively. However, measuring carbon saturation directly is only feasible in a laboratory setting, therefore, differential pressure has been used as a practical proxy. As the filter saturates, it impedes water flow, increasing the pressure difference and, by monitoring this difference, the system provides an accurate and real-time assessment of filter status.)
/E (To accurately monitor the effectiveness of water filters, the system employs a differential pressure sensor. This sensor features two pressure ports placed strategically before and after the filter. By measuring the inlet and outlet pressures, it can calculate the pressure difference. Based on this differential pressure and pre-determined laboratory values and assumptions, the system can determine whether the filter remains usable. This information is then conveyed to the user via an NFC tag. This choice of differential pressure to measure flow rate, rather than other parameters, is carefully considered: flow rate and the saturation of activated carbons are the only parameters that can reliably indicate filter effectiveness without requiring memory or a battery; activated carbons, which are widely used in water filters, absorb impurities through their micro-pores, once saturation is reached, they stop functioning effectively. However, measuring carbon saturation directly is only feasible in a laboratory setting, therefore, differential pressure has been used as a practical proxy. As the filter saturates, it impedes water flow, increasing the pressure difference and, by monitoring this difference, the system provides an accurate and real-time assessment of filter status.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
